WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.319 --> 00:00:01.913
(Robot whirring)

00:00:01.913 --> 00:00:04.756
- My name is Zoe Dingeman.

00:00:04.756 --> 00:00:06.633
(Robot whirring)

00:00:06.633 --> 00:00:08.599
I play football for Notre Dame.

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:11.287
(Suspenseful music)

00:00:11.287 --> 00:00:12.940
Robotic football.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:15.940
(suspenseful music)

00:00:26.416 --> 00:00:28.429
- [Narrator] At the
University of Notre Dame,

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:30.346
football is a big deal.

00:00:31.265 --> 00:00:34.177
In the basement halls of
the engineering school,

00:00:34.177 --> 00:00:36.574
a group of students are
turning their beloved

00:00:36.574 --> 00:00:38.157
sport into science.

00:00:39.061 --> 00:00:41.644
(upbeat music)

00:00:44.338 --> 00:00:46.998
- The Robotic Football
Club is an engineering club

00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:48.740
with students from a variety of different

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.199
engineering majors that work together

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:54.414
to design, build, fabricate,
and play with robots.

00:00:54.414 --> 00:00:56.169
(chattering)

00:00:56.169 --> 00:00:57.635
- You may not think it,
but robotic football

00:00:57.635 --> 00:00:59.285
actually is a lot like real football.

00:00:59.285 --> 00:01:02.290
Our goal in this club is to
make the game as comparable

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:05.001
to human football as possible.

00:01:05.001 --> 00:01:07.771
We have, for example, a
quarterback that can throw a ball.

00:01:07.771 --> 00:01:09.540
We have wide receivers
that can catch the ball.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.917
We have offensive linemen,
defensive linemen.

00:01:11.917 --> 00:01:13.208
Offensive linemen on the kickoff team,

00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:15.019
Connor and Michelle, I
want you hitting people

00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:15.918
on the kickoff.

00:01:15.918 --> 00:01:17.844
Robotic football is
definitely a contact sport.

00:01:17.844 --> 00:01:18.868
You're trying to hit
the other team's robots

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:20.505
as hard as you can,

00:01:20.505 --> 00:01:22.987
and so you need to build
designs that are robust

00:01:22.987 --> 00:01:24.093
that can handle that sort of stress

00:01:24.093 --> 00:01:25.807
that they're constantly placed under.

00:01:25.807 --> 00:01:28.930
(upbeat music)

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.142
- [Narrator] From it's
founding six years ago,

00:01:31.142 --> 00:01:34.486
the club's roster now boasts
20 robot football players

00:01:34.486 --> 00:01:37.165
at a variety of positions, all controlled

00:01:37.165 --> 00:01:40.498
and driven by 35 humans on the sideline.

00:01:42.179 --> 00:01:44.379
- [Zoe] This is RPM, it's
our first-string quarterback

00:01:44.379 --> 00:01:46.287
and it's a really interesting robot.

00:01:46.287 --> 00:01:49.508
It uses semi-autonomous
features to scan the field

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:51.975
and find the receiver
that it'd like to pass to.

00:01:51.975 --> 00:01:54.304
Once it does that, it can look
at the size of the receiver

00:01:54.304 --> 00:01:57.516
and know how far it should
throw the ball to it.

00:01:57.516 --> 00:01:59.526
This is Terabyte and
it's our running back.

00:01:59.526 --> 00:02:02.043
It's really great at finding
the gap to go down the field

00:02:02.043 --> 00:02:03.646
and get a touchdown.

00:02:03.646 --> 00:02:05.276
Irish Chocolate is one of our linebackers

00:02:05.276 --> 00:02:09.991
and it makes a lot of really
great open-field tackles.

00:02:09.991 --> 00:02:12.605
This is Justin Unit and it's our kicker.

00:02:12.605 --> 00:02:15.408
It can accurately kick the
ball between two and 60 feet.

00:02:15.408 --> 00:02:18.581
This is one of the most
precise robots on our team.

00:02:18.581 --> 00:02:21.362
(Celebratory music)

00:02:21.362 --> 00:02:23.392
- [Narrator] Notre Dame
competes against three other

00:02:23.392 --> 00:02:27.621
Midwest colleges in a
tournament held each spring,

00:02:27.621 --> 00:02:29.575
but it takes a full school year

00:02:29.575 --> 00:02:31.280
to prepare for that gauntlet.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:34.390
In the fall semester, the
team recruits new members

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.339
and trains them for a one-off scrimmage

00:02:36.339 --> 00:02:38.329
with another college's team.

00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:41.780
This year, their fall opponent
is Valparaiso University,

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.389
and they're not leaving
anything to chance.

00:02:44.389 --> 00:02:45.514
In the week before the game,

00:02:45.514 --> 00:02:47.281
they're running practice drills,

00:02:47.281 --> 00:02:48.586
they're learning the playbook,

00:02:48.586 --> 00:02:50.076
and they're watching tape,

00:02:50.076 --> 00:02:52.345
just like any other football team.

00:02:52.345 --> 00:02:53.892
- [Brendan] This is
basically the culmination

00:02:53.892 --> 00:02:55.621
of all this work you've put in in the shop

00:02:55.621 --> 00:02:56.521
with these robots, building them,

00:02:56.521 --> 00:02:59.022
repairing them, designing them.

00:02:59.022 --> 00:03:00.542
And you're going out
and basically watching

00:03:00.542 --> 00:03:03.482
your engineering put to the test.

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:04.315
Irish on three.

00:03:04.315 --> 00:03:05.148
One, two, three.

00:03:05.148 --> 00:03:06.531
- [Team] Irish.

00:03:06.531 --> 00:03:08.948
(applauding)

00:03:11.108 --> 00:03:13.691
(upbeat music)

00:03:15.154 --> 00:03:16.764
- [Zoe] The rules of robotic
football are very similar

00:03:16.764 --> 00:03:19.290
to that of human football,
so we follow NCAA rules

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.462
to the best of our ability.

00:03:21.462 --> 00:03:23.808
There are two 20 minute halves for a game,

00:03:23.808 --> 00:03:25.393
with a 10 minute half time,

00:03:25.393 --> 00:03:28.060
so it's a very quick-paced game.

00:03:29.503 --> 00:03:31.753
(cheering)

00:03:33.694 --> 00:03:35.366
- [Team] Yes. There we go.

00:03:35.366 --> 00:03:37.993
- [Zoe] These robots
take a lot of big hits

00:03:37.993 --> 00:03:41.404
(robots crashing)

00:03:41.404 --> 00:03:43.012
- We have a backup for every robot,

00:03:43.012 --> 00:03:45.029
just in case one breaks down.

00:03:45.029 --> 00:03:47.994
(yelling)

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:49.744
- [Announcer] This cannot be good.

00:03:49.744 --> 00:03:51.135
- [Zoe] So, if the quarterback breaks down

00:03:51.135 --> 00:03:53.587
and we pull it off and put
the second-string one in,

00:03:53.587 --> 00:03:55.253
and the pit-crew will work on it

00:03:55.253 --> 00:03:59.119
and try and fix it as quickly as possible.

00:03:59.119 --> 00:04:01.339
(suspenseful music)

00:04:01.339 --> 00:04:03.339
- [Narrator] With their starting
quarterback back in the game

00:04:03.339 --> 00:04:06.425
after a quick medical
break, the Robotic Irish

00:04:06.425 --> 00:04:10.700
fight to hold their narrow
lead but can't score.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:12.466
A touchdown and an extra point

00:04:12.466 --> 00:04:14.352
will snatch victory from Notre Dame.

00:04:14.352 --> 00:04:18.105
- [Team] This is the
game, this is the game.

00:04:18.105 --> 00:04:20.203
Come on, come on, come on, come on.

00:04:20.203 --> 00:04:22.339
Got it, got it, got it, got it.

00:04:22.339 --> 00:04:24.589
(Cheering)

00:04:29.676 --> 00:04:31.588
- [Zoe] The goal of this
is to grow it to be a truly

00:04:31.588 --> 00:04:32.902
national competition, where we can have

00:04:32.902 --> 00:04:35.623
multiple conferences
competing for the trophy.

00:04:35.623 --> 00:04:38.476
- [Narrator] One day, this
could be a real sport,

00:04:38.476 --> 00:04:40.310
or maybe it already is.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:41.143
- [Team] Irish on three.

00:04:41.143 --> 00:04:41.976
One, two, three.

00:04:41.976 --> 00:04:42.809
Irish.

00:04:42.809 --> 00:04:44.199
- [Brendan] This is
basically real football,

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:47.386
it's just not humans playing it.

00:04:47.386 --> 00:04:50.386
(suspenseful music)

